pair 2, 6 in pair 3): thin straight or diagonal strokes.\n- **Node colour** (2, 7 and 1 respectively): many small filled square blocks that the strokes connect.\n- **Endpoint colour** (1, 6 and 2 respectively): a square-block colour that occurs exactly twice.\n\nSo the picture is a network: blocks are stations and strokes are the links between them.\n\n**What changes.** Every changed cell becomes 5 (it was a line cell) or 3 (it was a node-block cell). In pair 1, the changed cells form one continuous route. It leaves the 1-block at the top right and runs down the 0-stroke at x=16. It then follows a diagonal to the 2-block at (13,10), continues along strokes to the 2-block at (14,4), and goes up to the other 1-block at (9,3).\n\n- Strokes on that route become 5.\n- Node blocks on it become 3.\n- The two endpoint blocks keep their colour.\n- Everything off the route is untouched, including dead-end branches.\n\nPairs 2 and 3 behave the same way, with the 6-blocks and the 2-blocks as the endpoints. So the rule is: **find the chain linking the two endpoint blocks, then recolour its strokes 5 and its intermediate blocks 3.**\n\n**How to compute it.**\n1. Work out the roles. A colour whose 4-connected components are all filled squares is a block colour; the other non-background colour is the line colour. Of the two block colours, the endpoint colour is the one with fewer blocks (exactly 2).\n2. Build a graph whose nodes are pieces:\n   - each block (a 4-connected component of a block colour);\n   - each stroke (an 8-connected component of the line colour, because strokes run diagonally).\n   Two pieces are joined if any of their cells touch, diagonals included.\n3. Run a breadth-first search from one endpoint block to the other through this graph. This gives the shortest chain of pieces.\n4. Recolour the pieces on that chain: strokes become 5 and intermediate blocks become 3.\n\n**Test input.** The two 3×3 blocks of 0 are the endpoints, the 3×3 blocks of 9 are the nodes, and the 2-wide 1 strokes are the lines. The program works out these roles from the grid itself, with no colours hard-coded. I have not run it against the train pairs yet.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    cols = [c for c in np.unique(g) if c != bg]\n\n    def is_square_comp(cells):\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = bbox(cells)\n        return (y1 - y0 == x1 - x0) and len(cells) == (y1 - y0 + 1) * (x1 - x0 + 1)\n\n    comps4 = {c: components(g == c) for c in cols}\n    block_cols = [c for c in cols if all(is_square_comp(cc) for cc in comps4[c])]\n    line_cols = [c for c in cols if c not in block_cols]\n    line_c = line_cols[0]\n    block_cols.sort(key=lambda c: len(comps4[c]))\n    end_c, node_c = block_cols[0], block_cols[1]\n\n    # build pieces\n    pieces = []  # (kind, cells)\n    for cc in comps4[end_c]:\n        pieces.append((\"end\", cc))\n    for cc in comps4[node_c]:\n        pieces.append((\"node\", cc))\n    for cc in components(g == line_c, diagonal=True):\n        pieces.append((\"line\", cc))\n    lab = -np.ones((H, W), int)\n    for i, (_, cc) in enumerate(pieces):\n        for y, x in cc:\n            lab[y, x] = i\n    adj = [set() for _ in pieces]\n    for y in range(H):\n        for x in range(W):\n            a = lab[y, x]\n            if a < 0:\n                continue\n            for dy in (-1, 0, 1):\n                for dx in (-1, 0, 1):\n                    ny, nx = y + dy, x + dx\n                    if 0 <= ny < H and 0 <= nx < W:\n                        b = lab[ny, nx]\n                        if b >= 0 and b != a:\n                            adj[a].add(b)\n\n    ends = [i for i, p in enumerate(pieces) if p[0] == \"end\"]\n    start, goal = ends[0], ends[1]\n    from collections import deque\n    prev = {start: None}\n    dq = deque([start])\n    while dq:\n        u = dq.popleft()\n        if u == goal:\n            break\n        for v in sorted(adj[u]):\n            if v in prev:\n                continue\n            # do not pass through the other endpoint unless it's the goal\n            if pieces[v][0] == \"end\" and v != goal:\n                continue\n            prev[v] = u\n            dq.append(v)\n\n    out = g.copy()\n    chain = []\n    u = goal if goal in prev else None\n    while u is not None:\n        chain.append(u)\n        u = prev[u]\n\n    for i in chain:\n        if pieces[i][0] == \"line\":\n            for y, x in pieces[i][1]:\n                out[y, x] = 5\n    emit(out.copy(), \"Recolour every stroke on the chain between the two endpoint blocks to 5.\")\n    for i in chain:\n        if pieces[i][0] == \"node\":\n            for y, x in pieces[i][1]:\n                out[y, x] = 3\n    emit(out.copy(), \"Recolour the intermediate node blocks on the chain to 3; endpoints keep their colour.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"objects":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"A colour whose 4-connected components are all filled squares is a block colour; the other non-background colour is the line colour."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- each block (a 4-connected component of a block colour);"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- each stroke (an 8-connected component of the line colour, because strokes run diagonally)."}],"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"This gives the shortest chain of pieces."}],"recolour":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"So the rule is: **find the chain linking the two endpoint blocks, then recolour its strokes 5 and its intermediate blocks 3.**"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Recolour the pieces on that chain: strokes become 5 and intermediate blocks become 3."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Recolour every stroke on the chain between the two endpoint blocks to 5."}],"paths":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Endpoint colour** (1, 6 and 2 respectively): a square-block colour that occurs exactly twice."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- The two endpoint blocks keep their colour."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Pairs 2 and 3 behave the same way, with the 6-blocks and the 2-blocks as the endpoints."}],"assembly":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Two pieces are joined if any of their cells touch, diagonals included."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"fe3c819d9d2d6f0e192deec8f36fcb5eb7628f0f7f5c33aa085dba38b0c20007","task_sha256":"adc34dafbad5ae94acac9b4dfe5cd9346f03a342a8c6c1e703c28ee72b3bb30c","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
